This package is a new extraction of Linux-visible System V IPC and POSIX
message-queue policy designed for TheKernel and distributed under Apache-2.0.
It is not a port of any upstream file: no Linux source file was translated,
adapted, or restructured here.

Linux sources were consulted to understand observable `msgrcv`/`msqctl`/
`shmctl` argument admission, `ipcperms()` DAC evaluation order, mqueue name
validation, and the errno each check produces.

Copied text: Linux C is quoted verbatim in Rust doc comments as the
observable-semantics authority. Measured against the Linux 7.2.3 tree
(whitespace-normalised lines of at least 40 characters inside fenced code
blocks, matched line for line): 4 lines across 3 blocks, all in `src/lib.rs`;
at the inclusive 25-character threshold 9 lines across the same 3 blocks. The
hunks are `fs/libfs.c:69-72` (`simple_lookup()`, at `src/lib.rs:124-129`),
`fs/namei.c:198-206` (`do_getname()`, at `:139-149`), and `fs/namei.c:3094-3101`
(`lookup_noperm_common()`, at `:156-165`).

The prose around each block names the Linux file and the enclosing Linux
function but gives no line range, and no block in this package carries the
inline `Excerpt: Linux v7.2.3 <file>:<lines> - GPL-2.0-only,
(C) The Linux Kernel Authors` marker that `net`, `mount`, `aio`, and `random`
use. Adding the ranges above and the markers is a source-side follow-up owned by
this package; the ranges stated here were derived by matching the quoted lines
against the 7.2.3 tree. Other Linux ranges cited in this package
(`ipc/util.c:553`, `kernel/groups.c:92`, `:227`, `security/security.c:3370`) are
prose citations of code reimplemented in Rust, not copies.

Those fragments are GPL-2.0-only, (C) The Linux Kernel Authors. They are
comment text, not code: no Linux code is linked, assembled, or compiled into
this package, and the Apache-2.0 grant above does not extend to the quoted
text. Nothing else in this package reproduces upstream source text. See
`docs/upstream-provenance.md` for the full per-file index.
